# macboot — global git ignore, linked to ~/.config/git/ignore by scripts/50-git.sh
# and pinned as core.excludesfile in git/gitconfig.
#
# Lowest precedence of all ignore sources: a repo's own .gitignore, its
# .git/info/exclude and any deeper .gitignore all override what is here, so a
# project that WANTS one of these files only has to say so (`!.idea/`). That is
# also why this file stays short — a rule here applies to every repo on the disk,
# including ones you clone from other people.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The reason this file exists at all
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Claude Code writes .claude/settings.local.json into whatever directory you run
# it in. It holds per-machine permission grants — local paths, allowed commands,
# sometimes an approved curl target — and it is regenerated constantly, so it
# shows up as a new untracked file in every repo you touch and eventually rides
# along in a `git add -A`.
#
# On the source machine the ONLY thing preventing that was a 31-byte global
# ignore, sitting at a path nobody would think to look at, holding this one line.
# Losing that file would have been silent: nothing errors, the file just starts
# appearing in `git status` again. It is therefore tracked here, first, with this
# explanation attached.
#
# The `**/` is load-bearing. Per gitignore(5), a pattern containing a slash anywhere
# except at the end is anchored to the ignore file's directory level -- and for
# core.excludesFile that level is the TOP OF THE WORKING TREE. So the bare
# `.claude/settings.local.json` matched only a repo root, and `sub/.claude/...` or
# `apps/web/.claude/...` were NOT ignored (verified with `git check-ignore -v`). Since
# running Claude Code from a subdirectory of a monorepo is the normal way it is used,
# the one pattern this whole file exists for was failing in the common case. Every
# other entry here (.idea/, .venv/, __pycache__/, node_modules/) has no internal slash
# and so already matched at any depth.
**/.claude/settings.local.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# macOS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Finder writes one per directory it has ever displayed, including directories
# inside a checkout you opened once to look at a screenshot.
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?

# AppleDouble resource forks, created when a file is copied to a filesystem
# without native xattr support (a USB stick, a network share) and copied back.
._*
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editor droppings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# JetBrains project metadata: absolute paths, local run configurations and a
# per-user workspace.xml that conflicts on literally every merge.
.idea/
*.iml

# vim swap files, present only while a buffer is open — long enough to be caught
# by `git add -A` during an edit.
*.swp
*.swo
.*.sw[a-p]
*~

# Deliberately NOT here: .vscode/. Ignoring a whole directory globally makes it
# impossible for a repo to re-include a single file inside it (git cannot
# re-include a path whose parent directory is excluded), and shared
# .vscode/extensions.json or launch.json are worth committing. Repos that want
# their .vscode private can say so in their own .gitignore.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Language and tool caches — big, machine-specific, always regenerable
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# uv creates .venv by convention. Committing one publishes absolute
# interpreter paths and compiled wheels for exactly one machine and OS.
.venv/
venv/

# Bytecode. Stale .pyc files that shadow a deleted module are a genuinely
# confusing class of bug, and one you inflict on whoever clones the repo.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Tool caches keyed to absolute paths on this machine.
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# node_modules is per-platform (native addons) and reproducible from the lockfile,
# which is the file that should be committed instead.
node_modules/

# direnv's materialised environment, not its .envrc.
.direnv/
